<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad al-Idrisi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1100–c. 1165</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as al-Idrisi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad al-Idrisi was an Arab geographer and cartographer, born probably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a>, who worked at the court of the Norman king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>. In 1154 he completed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabula-rogeriana">Tabula Rogeriana</a>, or Book of Roger, a description of the known world with a world map divided into climatic zones. It was one of the most detailed geographical works of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-idrisi.jpg" alt="Muhammad al-Idrisi" width="320" height="474" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Estatua_de_Al-Idrisi_bajo_el_baluarte_de_los_Mallorquines,_Ceuta_(3).jpg: Mario Sánchez Bueno derivative work: Ecemaml · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Estatua_de_Al-Idrisi_bajo_el_baluarte_de_los_Mallorquines,_Ceuta_(5).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
